export const homepageIntroductionHtml = "\u003cp\u003eHi there, it's my pleasure to greet you here, in my first intiated website (very informal language, this is my style because I feel that being lofty and intimidating is never my purpose of designing this website). So, as you see, this is the brief intro of my website, and let's get started.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEmm, let's start with the explanation of the English name of this website. If you click on the underlined \"e\", an animation will takes up the entire screen changing \"e\" to \"i\"; by clicking the underlined \"i\", it jumps back to the \"i\" with a formula that you might be familiar with (doesn't matter if you do not recognize it, it's Euler's formula). This small replacement of one letter changes the overall meaning of the word significantly: antemundane means before the formation of the universe/world; antimundane means challenging convention and traditional values. And the \"sprǣc\" is the old English expression of communication. Hence, from a glimpse of my website title, the basic design purpose is showcased: I aim to construct a community that welcomes any individual interested in physics, mathematics, or philosophy to participate in in-depth discussions revealing fascinating feature of each of these branch of interest.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHere, you may discover not only knowledge of each branch of interest and discuss with others but various fun functionalities of the website as well. We have a journal subbranch which will periodically uploads new articles or links of academic articles from Internet (yet articles demanding insitituitonal logins may not be available, the journal section overally requires further experiments). Besides, we have forums for free discussions of each branch of interest aside with fun games related to each branch (i.e. a physics parabola bombard game in which you need to adjust the angle according to the air flow and the relative position; a mathematic game in which you need to first decide your coordinate and then create your function to achieve certain purposes; a philosophical game in which you will encounter distinctive moral dilemmas).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd more? Yes! Our website is not static, it will transplant a Desmos graphical calculator soon, and later on our website's own simulations. More interesting utilities are waiting for you to propose, design, and enjoy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis is not a normal subject study website but a zone for you to engage with freely discussing topics of branch of interests that are beyond the textbook scope. I sincerely expect that everyone involved enjoys and provides us with your own energy. Hope you enjoy, and take your time.\u003c/p\u003e\n";

export const physicsIntroductionHtml = "\u003cp\u003ePhysics is (one of) the most fascinating science subject(s), delete the content in the brackets, huh huh. As you've clicked on this website's URL, you've probably noticed that this website sorts information into three categories: Physics, Mathematics, Philosophy. And, obviously, I, as the designer and initiator of this webiste, aim to reveal and reinforce the connection between each two of them. Yes, they're strongly correlated subjects--anyway, I indeed hate the diction of \"subject\" which sounds like a strong \"school\" vocab. Despite that most scientific research is conducted in educational institutions, it's very different from a traditional school. Thus, I would rather name it as a branch of scientific interest instead of subject. \u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo, what is physics? I guess there are certainly some great explanations linked to the English diction of physics--probably originated from Ancient Greek? Yet I do prefer to explain physics utilizing Chinese: physics's Chinese name is \"物理\", which means \"the law/principle of everything in nature\". Right, the subject of research of physics is everthing in our universe, and this branch of interest is deeply related to philosophy. Consider this question, \"is science a religion?\" If I were a primary school student, I may naively answer \"no\". Nevertheless, in recent years through closer study, I would answer this question with \"yes\". The origin of physics is human beings' common curiosity and consequential thoughts which bring conclusions as knowledge of the world. Where do we come from? What's the universe? What are those stars on the night sky? What's us made of? Asking interesting questions is the very start.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eQuestions; thoughts; observations (intuition); assumptions (theories); experiments; analysis; models. This is the common order of physics research, in which observation can be at any place (and can be potentially part of experiment). Sometimes, this order is not followed, yet it's a general order of modern physics research. Honestly, as any other branch of interest, the way of physics research evolves over time: in the past, physicists probably began with observation of reoccuring daily phenomenons: objects always fall from high to low position; to ensure one thing always move, you need to push it; river freezes in winter. Based on these life experiences, one forms its own intuition (as repeating pattern is gradually imprinted in our mind, and when we make a prediction, i.e. we are afraid of falling from the height beyond the usual height of the second floor, because our experience tells us that we will fall down with an increasing speed and get injured.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, intuition sometimes may be challenged and may be misleading. Suppose that you've seen hundreds of thousands of times of various objects falling down to the ground, you find that magically--when releasing at the same height--two different objects hit the ground floor at the same time. But, when you accidentally release a feather from certain height, compared with a rock, something challenging your intuition appears--the feather swirls gently with wind and slowly reaches the ground, while the rock rapidly hit the ground with a huge sound of impact. Then, you come up with an updated version of intuition that heavier matter reaches the ground with a shorter period of time when compared with lighter stuff. Nonetheless, have you considered the effect of air? When you open the side window of your vehicle, leaning your hand out of the car, you feel a resistance against your direction of moving car. The sense of resistance becomes stronger when the car becomes faster, and vice versa. Oh, so we need to consider the effect of air, resistance? \u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNever fear of the upcoming day on which your intuition is challenged. As we've demonstrated, intuiton is plastic, indicating that it's not immutable truth. When our experience cannot be applied, we shall think about some omitted but vital perspectives that haven't been included. As time goes on, our intuiton develops and becomes more universal and prevalent. So is physics. Physics is never the real world itself but the evolving models that simulate and approximate our real world. From Aristotle to Galileo and Newton; from Maxwell to Einstein and Bohr, physics models and theories are challenged and updated through effort of each new generation that's injected into the vein of physics. The study of physics is every effort made to better the existing model, rendering it to be capable of explaining phenomenons in a broader scope and predicting things more precisely (when mentioning precision, I always refer to the development of astronomy over time, which is highly associated with the evolution of theory of gravitational force. It's a very interesting topic that we will discuss later on). \u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen, what's the significance and meaning of figuring out all these simulations? Some will argue that I rarely encounter the scenarios that highly demand the knowledge of physics in my daily life. You're both right and wrong. Granted, we don't meet those situations often in life, but we're manipulating the knowledge of physics without awaring it. That's your intuition integrated with our senses. And, from a more general and broader perspective, every progress of physics accelerates the global development of human civilizations through history. From industrial revolution to nowadays chips that we rely on to run computer games smoothly, any engineering project must begin with the premise of possessing related physics knowledge.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd, for people stating that they generally consider physics as intimidating, or plain, or less magnetic branch of interest, I strongly recommend for giving it a chance. Probably, it's simply that you haven't met the fascinating part of it. Remember, this is not conventional classroom, you're allowed to think freely and saunter based on your own will. Anyway, take your time.\u003c/p\u003e\n";

export const mathematicsIntroductionHtml = "\u003cp\u003eDear guest, welcome to mathematics space, a place for free exploration and discussions. As I've mentioned in the homepage overview intro, the functonality of this website is concise: free exploration of specific branch of interest, so is the mathematics page. This is neither a conventional class nor a textbook with intimidating equations and tedious narrative; instead, this space is more about having fun than obtaining better grades, which is suggested by our webpage title as well.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe origin of math is human beings' logical thinking: our ancestors invented number system for recording events (i.e. calculating the total number of preys collected), and the calculation operation evolved over time to better adapt to more complex scenarios, thus yielding addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. As time went on, the number system was gradually integrated with things beyond the daily trade application: Ancient Egyptians invented the most basic and primary astronomy; measurements of distances, angles, weights, etc became more prevalent and demanded the involvement of mathematic system. This is how math connects to our life, with numbers (algebra) reflected by the shapes (geometry).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen, we may know the story that Issac Newton was hit by a falling apple from the tree (which is, to large extent, a fake story oversimplifies the process), accidentally coming up with the universal law of gravitational force along with mathematical technique named calculus. And another man named Leibniz proclaimed that he also invented calculus independently. Thus, the deep interconnection has been built between mathematics and physics. They're like the two children of human beings' mind: one's born and raised up by pure logical derivation, while the other originates from the ingrained personal philosophy. One distinction is that physics possesses its own falsifiablity (indicating that it can be falsified and rectified), yet mathematics is not. This does not promise mathematic with a peaceful and steady progression, though. There are serveral mathematics crisis taking place through history, with great progress established by overcoming each crisis and reaching compromise.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd as years passed, the mathematics became more sophisticated, with tons of new theories and increasing abstraction through the continuous division of various subbranches. One potential crisis occurs for both math and physics: artificial intelligence. Currently, AI can replace most of the advanced theoretica field research, risking each passionate mathematicians' future. Nevertheless, a revealed inertia of all those mathematicans and physicists appears: we're intentionally or unintentionally rendering advanced research to become lofty to avoid being judged by others. Suppose that you're a mathematician in a specific research field, probably fluid dynamics, but you may hardly understand your peers' report with only slight deviation from your research direction. Branching too much so that only a small portion of people can get access to your research result, since others are being rejected by your intimidating yet meaningless math notations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis pretension and lack of social responsibility shall be forbiddened with zero allowance. Mathematics can never detach too much from our real life, regardless of the level of complexity of the field (i.e. topography). Complicating problem and creating new terminologies will never work. We need cooperation and ignorance of reputations and other rewards associated with new research accomplishment. Thus, in brief, our website wants to cultivate real passion of subject, regardless of grades and any other assigned social values. We're doing math simply because that we love math and believe in the power of math which can reshape our community through efforts (making a meaningful and impressive difference to our world). We're not someone seeking for prizes and associated awards of money.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd surely I understand that some of our community members were once frustrated by their learning and exploration experience of math. But remeber, you're no longer in a classroom but in a free space in which you can explore more than textbooks and quiz papers. This website is like a digital library as well, welcoming everyone to step in to take a breath of fresh air of math and find a book to sit with. So, take your time and enjoy.\u003c/p\u003e\n";

export const philosophyIntroductionHtml = "\u003cp\u003eWelcome to the philosophy site! It seems to be unrelevant to any scientific branch, yet it indeed gestates and cultivates physics and math. And at the very beginning as you read this line, I want to clarify that philosophy, as the two parallel branches of interest, is not privelege of minority elites--in reality, philosophy might be the most fundamental element of individuals' life that everyone has already become a philosopher without awaring it explicitly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePhilosophy is a broad area of study sustaining and gratifying our human kind's curiosity, and I'm glad that I've put religion as one independent subbranch of philosophy. One interesting question asked is: is science a religion? And, currently, my answer is \"yes\". Like religion and science, philosophy was initiated by our ancestors driven by the common curiosity about the unknown and unrevealed secrets of the world. Where do we come from? What's our soul? Where's our destination after death? What is free will? What's our universe made of? How enormous is it? All these questions were proposed and then answered by individuals from their own perspectives, creating the very inital philosophy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGradually, some questions developed into more specific branch and entangled with physics and math (or, in some way, they're the origin of physics and math). If human beings' mind were a continuous flow of time, some of it would become sediments and sank to the bottom with slight forward motion that sometimes still continued with the river flow yet much slower. These sediments belong to science, and the remaining ones are still philosophical questions. If you're interested in searching Ancient Greek philosophers on Wikipedia, you would probably end up finding that most of them also contributed to the early development of physics (i.e. Aristotle stated that objects' motion need a constant \"force\" to maintain. Though we know that this conclusion is not accurate, it's understandable due to the limited knowledge at that era). These were once philosophical questions which later became converted into physics questions with certain answers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOr, some of them will always lack a certain answer. Yes, to some extent, science does have certain answers for solved problems (not in all cases, though); but philosophical questions do not have certain \"yes\" or \"no\" questions. Individuals like you and I certainly may have our own different certain answers, yet there's no one finite answer (i.e. the train problem). And this is the charisma of philosophy--the openess to everyone with their own perspectives. Sometimes, a question may be so complicated that it exceeds the scope of a normal binary mode: with a slight distinction, the resulted answer may be vastly different.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnother charisma of philosophy is that there's no academic gap and domination of authorities, since, as I've stated, everyone can be a philosopher, from the most fundamental life philsophy to moral decisions and attitudes. Famous philosophers do remain their positions through history of philosophy development, yet they're waiting there for us to reach to them and begin a brand new conversation or debate over certain fascinating topics. Despite that their mortal flesh decayed, the soul of theirs remains, stays alive, and interacts vividly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBesides, a notable fact is that human beings' intelligence seems to stay stagnant over thousands of years that if our ancestor (someone that you may consider as outdated and primordial person can well adapt to modern society in a short period of time). We know that people of different eras share little of mundane topics due to limitation of each era (or, we clearly conceive that Confucianism cannot be fully adapted to modern society's context); yet, in general, there are something as the previously mentioned common curiosity that's under the surface theory--something that's universally shared. Thus, the conversation, as the website title suggests, is a conversation between souls regardless of any material possessions. More importantly, it's a conversation surpassing the limitation of time, connecting past, present, and future.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePhilosophy is not always discussing severe problems and extreme situations; sometimes, it's more about some fun topics deserving in-depth dicussions and a closer look. And surely someone may blame philosophy that it's very abstract and detached from real life (and ridiculously their judgement is based on the reality that students of philosophy major generally possess a poor employment rate). Granted, philosophy seems to be apart from reality, but the product of philosophical thinking is around us: the commonly accepted morality/ethics; updated legislations; questions that attract generations' attention which ultimately lead to social progress with new technologies developed and dilemma avoided.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTherefore, I suspect the justification of your reasons for not touching philosophy. It'll be an impressive and \"startling\" journey, I promise. Enjoy!\u003c/p\u003e\n";

